From 3f8c8ddffb4dbd49c5a2f26db4f193983ac76859 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jarojarojaro-jaro <miki.jaroszek@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 17:59:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 33/54] ggml/llama: delta-net speculative rollback by replay of
 a lagged state (GATED_DELTA_NET_LAG)

The qwen35 GDN verify wrote a full state snapshot per token (5 x 3 MiB per layer at MTP n=4) so that any prefix
could be restored. The cache now keeps one state per cell that lags behind the cell position by the rows of the
previous ubatch: their post-norm k, v, g and beta are kept in a pending buffer (two planes) and the next ubatch's
GDN kernel replays the still-kept rows with the token loop's arithmetic before running its own rows, then writes
the state once. A rollback of r tokens is rs_pend_n -= r on the host. The conv state keeps its snapshots.

- ggml: GGML_OP_GATED_DELTA_NET_LAG (CPU reference, CUDA/HIP kernel variant of gated_delta_net_cuda, fusions of
  the GDN prologue / state gather / cache cpy apply as before); test-backend-ops GATED_DELTA_NET_LAG
- llama: llama_memory_recurrent rs_lag (single sequence, offloaded states, device support probed), s_l with one
  row per cell, pend_l per layer, seq_rm rollback, state save/load of the pending rows
- off-switch LLAMA_GDN_REPLAY=0 (or GGML_HIP_GDN_REPLAY=0)
